{
    "background_story": "Enoch was a rugged geologist who spent his life studying the unique landscapes of the American West. His fascination with the scablands of eastern Washington led him to dedicate years to researching the region's fascinating geological history. Enoch's expeditions took him through the vast, barren expanses of the Channeled Scablands, where he marveled at the eerie beauty of the landscape carved by ancient floods. As he explored the area, he began to unravel the secrets of the region's formation, publishing papers that shed new light on the catastrophic events that shaped the scablands. Enoch's work not only advanced the field of geology but also inspired a new generation of scientists to explore the natural wonders of the scablands. His name became synonymous with the region, and his legacy continued to inspire others to venture into the vast, mysterious expanse of the scablands.",
